    "created_at": "2024-03-21T20:34:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2024-03-21T20:34:36Z",
    "body": "> But it's likely that the feature relies on some not-upstreamed Edge-only backend, as the relevant CLs  ([1](https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/5247315), [2](https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/5297548)) don't really add any functionality related to the attribute. So maybe [Edge Canary](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/edge/download/insider?form=MA13FJ) is the way to go for testing this.\r\n\r\n\r\nThat's right -- [Edge Canary](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/edge/download/insider) is the thing to test with here.\r\n\r\nFor clarity, there are two things being discussed in this thread that are related but not the same:\r\n\r\n- The `writingsuggestions` attribute, newly standardized and shipping in Chrome and Edge 124. This can be used by authors as a signal to browsers to turn off any browser-native writing suggestions features that the platform may have implemented. The details of these writing suggestions features are left up to browsers. Chrome/Chromium does not currently implement any such feature, so `writingsuggestions` is effectively a no-op there for the time being.\r\n- The Edge [text prediction feature](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/edge/features/text-prediction). This has already existed in Edge for a while, but going forward web authors will be able to turn it off selectively with the `writingsuggestions` attribute.",
